[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186162663 :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=25.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-25I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-25I=[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161270 :answer-field/correct 17592186161271 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fb373261-4ba5-5be3-91b7-f2e6612a259f" :activity/id #uuid "fb373261-4ba5-5be3-91b7-f2e6612a259f" :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type :activity